---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
const versionOut = `osewa 0.1.0 (protocol 0, ghostty 83fb9562…, snapshot v1)`;
const versionJson = `{"ghostty":"83fb9562…","osewa":"0.1.0","snapshot_version":1,"version":0}`;
---
<Docs slug="cli" description="Every osewa command and flag: login, attach, ls, new, kill, exec, forward, update, version, server, service and enroll.">
<p class="lede">One binary, two sets of commands: client commands on your machine, host commands on the server. Flags may go anywhere among the arguments, and <code>-flag</code> works as well as <code>--flag</code>.</p>

<h2>Client commands</h2>

<h3 id="login">osewa login &lt;ssh-target&gt;</h3>
<p>Install osewa on a host over ssh, enroll this device and start the server. See <a href="/docs/install">Install and log in</a>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>--service</code></td><td>Run the server as a user service.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--name N</code></td><td>The host's name here. Default: the target without <code>user@</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--addr host:port</code></td><td>Where to dial it. Default: ssh's hostname for the target, port 7322.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--device N</code></td><td>This device's name on the host. Default: this machine's hostname.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--no-install</code></td><td>Use the osewa already on the host's <code>PATH</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--no-check</code></td><td>Don't dial the host before saving it.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="attach">osewa attach [flags] &lt;host&gt; [id]</h3>
<p>Attach to a session. Without an id: the only session, or a new one if there are none. See <a href="/docs/sessions">Sessions</a>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>--take</code></td><td>Take ownership from another device.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--predict auto|always|never</code></td><td>Local echo prediction. Default <code>auto</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--graphics auto|always|never</code></td><td>Kitty graphics. Default <code>auto</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--clipboard-read</code></td><td>Let host programs read your clipboard.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--quiet</code></td><td>No bottom-row notices when an agent is blocked or done.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Inside: <code>~.</code> detaches, <code>~t</code> or Ctrl-T (as a viewer) takes ownership, <code>~~</code> sends a <code>~</code>. Escapes work after Enter.</p>

<h3 id="ls">osewa ls &lt;host&gt;</h3>
<p>List sessions: <code>ID TITLE SIZE OWNER ATTACHED IDLE STATUS STATE</code>. Columns are padded with spaces, so scripts can split them.</p>

<h3 id="new">osewa new &lt;host&gt; [-- argv...]</h3>
<p>Start a session without attaching, and print its id. Runs your login shell, or <code>argv</code>.</p>

<h3 id="kill">osewa kill &lt;host&gt; &lt;id&gt;</h3>
<p>End a session.</p>

<h3 id="exec">osewa exec [--cwd dir] [--env K=V]... &lt;host&gt; -- argv...</h3>
<p>Run a command on the host without a terminal; exits with its code. See <a href="/docs/forward-exec">Forwards and exec</a>.</p>

<h3 id="forward">osewa forward &lt;host&gt; [bind:]port[:host:hostport]...</h3>
<p>Forward local ports through the host, as <code>ssh -L</code>, until Ctrl-C.</p>

<h3 id="update">osewa update &lt;host&gt;</h3>
<p>Copy this build to the host over osewa and restart its server with it. See <a href="/docs/server">Running the server</a>.</p>

<h3 id="version">osewa version [--json]</h3>
<pre><code><span class="p">$ </span>osewa version
{versionOut}
<span class="p">$ </span>osewa version --json
{versionJson}</code></pre>
<p>Two builds work together when <code>version</code> (the wire protocol), <code>ghostty</code> and <code>snapshot_version</code> all match.</p>

<h2>Host commands</h2>

<h3 id="server">osewa server [flags]</h3>
<p>Serve sessions to enrolled devices. <code>--daemon</code> detaches into the background.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>--listen :7322</code></td><td>Address and port, TCP and UDP.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--name N</code></td><td>The server's name. Default: the hostname.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--daemon</code></td><td>Run in the background, logging to <code>~/.local/state/osewa/server.log</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--no-quic</code></td><td>TLS WebSocket only.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--no-compress</code></td><td>Don't compress WebSocket traffic.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--no-forward</code></td><td>Refuse port forwards.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>--no-exec</code></td><td>Refuse commands without a terminal.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="service">osewa service install [server flags] | uninstall</h3>
<p>Run the server as a systemd (Linux) or launchd (macOS) user service, or remove it. <code>install</code> takes the server flags above, except <code>--daemon</code>.</p>

<h3 id="enroll">osewa enroll [--port N]</h3>
<p>Add a device. login runs this over ssh, with the request on stdin; you don't run it by hand. <code>--port</code> is the port it tells the device to dial (default 7322).</p>

<h3>osewa holder</h3>
<p>One session's process. The server starts it; you never do.</p>

<h2>Exit codes</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Success.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>An error, printed as <code>osewa &lt;command&gt;: &lt;message&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Bad arguments.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>other</td><td><code>attach</code> and <code>exec</code> exit with the remote program's code.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
</Docs>
